---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArrowIcon from '../components/ArrowIcon.astro';
import SectionEyebrow from '../components/SectionEyebrow.astro';
import '../styles/curriculum.css';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Curriculum"
  navCurrent="curriculum"
  description="Our curriculum is built on Australia's Early Years Learning Framework and structured around the five developmental domains used nationally to track how children are growing."
>

  {/* ── Page header ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="page-header-inner">
      <SectionEyebrow number="00" label="Curriculum" />
      <h1 class="display">
        Built on the Early Years Learning Framework.<br />
        Grounded in <em>how children actually grow</em>.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Our curriculum is guided by Australia's national <strong>Early Years
        Learning Framework (EYLF, 2009)</strong> and aligned with the
        <strong>Australian Early Development Census (AEDC)</strong> — the same
        five developmental domains used nationally to understand how children
        are tracking by the time they start school.
      </p>
      <p class="lede lede-secondary">
        Every day here, we're intentionally supporting your child across
        <strong>five domains</strong> — physical, social, emotional, language,
        and cognitive — through both structured learning and child-led
        exploration. This page sets out what we do in each, and the thinking
        skills we build through play.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  {/* ── 01 · The Five Developmental Domains ─────────────── */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <SectionEyebrow number="01" label="The five developmental domains" />
      <h2 class="display">
        What we develop in <em>every child</em>, every day.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="domains">

      {/* ── i. Physical ── */}
      <article class="domain">
        <span class="domain-num">i.</span>
        <h3>Physical Development</h3>
        <p class="domain-tagline">Growing strong, capable bodies — indoors and out.</p>
        <div class="domain-body">
          <p>
            We support gross-motor development through climbing, running,
            balancing, jumping, digging, riding, and outdoor play in nature.
            Fine-motor skills are built through threading, cutting, drawing,
            painting, construction, cooking, and practical-life activities like
            pouring, buttoning, and self-care.
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="vignette">
          <span class="vignette-label">What this looks like</span>
          <p>
            A child negotiates the climbing frame, decides which hold to try
            next, and discovers their own edge of "safe but stretching." Inside,
            the same child practises threading beads onto a string — the same
            focus, smaller muscles, the same satisfaction.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </article>

      {/* ── ii. Social ── */}
      <article class="domain">
        <span class="domain-num">ii.</span>
        <h3>Social Development</h3>
        <p class="domain-tagline">Learning to belong, share, and build friendships.</p>
        <div class="domain-body">
          <p>
            Children practise turn-taking, cooperation, negotiation, conflict
            resolution, and being part of a group. Mixed-age interactions give
            older children a chance to lead and younger children a chance to
            learn through observation. We deliberately scaffold the small
            social moments — joining a game, repairing a fall-out, including a
            quieter friend — because these are taught, not just absorbed.
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="vignette">
          <span class="vignette-label">What this looks like</span>
          <p>
            Two children both want the last truck. Instead of intervening to
            decide, an educator stays close and helps each child name what they
            want, listen to the other, and find a way through. Next time, they
            have a little more of the skill themselves.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </article>

      {/* ── iii. Emotional ── */}
      <article class="domain">
        <span class="domain-num">iii.</span>
        <h3>Emotional Development</h3>
        <p class="domain-tagline">Knowing how you feel, and what to do about it.</p>
        <div class="domain-body">
          <p>
            We help children recognise and name their feelings, manage
            frustration, build confidence, develop a sense of identity, and
            grow the resilience to try again after a setback. Emotional
            regulation is treated as a learnable skill, not a character trait —
            and it's modelled by every adult in the room.
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="vignette">
          <span class="vignette-label">What this looks like</span>
          <p>
            A child whose tower falls for the third time looks ready to cry. An
            educator sits beside them and says, "That's frustrating, isn't it?
            You worked hard on it." The child takes a breath — and reaches for
            another block.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </article>

      {/* ── iv. Language ── */}
      <article class="domain">
        <span class="domain-num">iv.</span>
        <h3>Language Development</h3>
        <p class="domain-tagline">Words for thinking, words for connecting.</p>
        <div class="domain-body">
          <p>
            We support listening, speaking, vocabulary, storytelling, early
            literacy, and print awareness through conversation, songs, rhymes,
            storytime, dramatic play, and questioning. Children whose home
            language isn't English are supported in both, because their first
            language is part of who they are.
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="vignette">
          <span class="vignette-label">What this looks like</span>
          <p>
            During a planting activity, an educator narrates and extends —
            "You're patting the soil down. Why do you think the seed needs to
            be covered?" The child stops, thinks, and offers a theory. The
            conversation is the lesson.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </article>

      {/* ── v. Cognitive ── */}
      <article class="domain domain-cognitive">
        <span class="domain-num">v.</span>
        <h3>Cognitive Development</h3>
        <p class="domain-tagline">Building curious, capable thinkers.</p>
        <div class="domain-body">
          <p>
            This is the broadest of the five domains, and the one our
            play-and-inquiry approach develops most deliberately. It covers how
            children investigate the world, solve problems, and make sense of
            what they discover.
          </p>

          <div class="subdomain">
            <h4>STEM and mathematical thinking</h4>
            <ul class="subdomain-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Science.</strong> Hands-on experiments with water,
                sand, light, shadow, magnets, plants, mini-beasts. Asking
                questions about the natural world, making predictions, and
                testing them.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Physics in play.</strong> Ramps and balls (force,
                motion, gravity), balance and construction, pulleys and levers
                in the playground.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Biology and life sciences.</strong> Growing food,
                observing insects and birds, caring for plants, exploring the
                lifecycles of butterflies and frogs.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Mathematics.</strong> Counting, measuring, weighing,
                exploring volume, time, shape, and positional language ("under,"
                "between," "behind"). Maths shows up in cooking, building,
                packing away, and games — not just in worksheets.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="subdomain">
            <h4>Technology and digital literacy</h4>
            <p>
              Age-appropriate exposure to simple tools and how things work —
              magnifiers, weighing scales, timers, basic coding-style sequence
              games, photography of their own discoveries. Technology is a tool
              for inquiry, never a babysitter.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="subdomain">
            <h4>Language as thinking</h4>
            <p>
              Spoken and emerging written language is also a cognitive tool —
              it's how children organise ideas, ask questions, and reason out
              loud.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 02 · Thinking processes ─────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section section-thinking">
    <div class="section-head">
      <SectionEyebrow number="02" label="The thinking processes" />
      <h2 class="display">
        The cognitive habits that <em>outlast any single fact</em>.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <figure class="research-quote research-quote-inline">
      <blockquote>
        "Creative experiences should require children to use a range of
        different thinking processes to achieve a goal."
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>— Mayesky, 2006</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="thinking-intro">
      We design play and learning experiences so children regularly practise
      the full range of thinking skills described in the EYLF (2009) and in
      Mayesky's work on creative experiences. These are the cognitive habits
      that outlast any single fact a child might learn.
    </p>

    <ol class="thinking-list">
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Observation</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Noticing carefully, paying attention to detail.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Comparing &amp; classifying</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Sorting, grouping, finding patterns.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Problem-solving</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Working out how to get something to work.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Inquiring</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Asking questions and pursuing the answers.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Hypothesising</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Making thoughtful guesses about what might happen.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Investigating</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Exploring something in depth to understand it.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Experimenting</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Trying things out, noticing what changes.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Researching</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Gathering information from books, people, and the environment.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="thinking-name">Making decisions</span>
        <span class="thinking-explainer">Choosing between options and acting on the choice.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="cross-link">
      These nine processes are <em>what</em> good thinking looks like. Our
      <a href="/#curriculum">EXPLORE methodology</a> is <em>how</em> we cultivate
      them in the rhythm of an ordinary day.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── 03 · Metacognition ──────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section-meta">
    <div class="section-meta-inner">
      <div class="section-head">
        <SectionEyebrow number="03" label="Metacognition" />
        <h2 class="display">
          Teaching children to <em>think about their thinking</em>.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <p class="meta-intro">
        From around the age of three, children become capable of one of the
        most powerful cognitive skills there is: <strong>metacognition</strong> —
        thinking about thinking. We teach it intentionally, because the
        research is clear about how much it matters.
      </p>

      <figure class="research-quote research-quote-feature">
        <blockquote>
          "Young children using metacognitive abilities and behaviours learn
          and remember more efficiently than others and become more strategic,
          flexible, and productive in their learning process."
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>— Chatzipantelia, Grammatikopoulos &amp; Gregoriadis, 2014</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="meta-body">
        The NSW Government (2020) describes metacognition as behaviour directly
        linked with a person's control and monitoring of their own learning,
        including:
      </p>

      <ul class="meta-behaviours">
        <li>Setting goals</li>
        <li>Making sure a problem is understood before answering</li>
        <li>Monitoring memory recall and comprehension</li>
        <li>Reflection</li>
        <li>Generating and testing hypotheses</li>
        <li>Self-questioning</li>
        <li>Brainstorming ideas</li>
        <li>Strategy selection</li>
      </ul>

      <h4 class="meta-how">How we teach it</h4>
      <p class="meta-body">
        Through hands-on experiences and ongoing feedback. We name the thinking
        children are doing while they're doing it — <em>"You tried one way,
        that didn't work, and now you're trying a different way. That's good
        thinking."</em> After an activity, we reflect together: <em>"What did
        you try? What worked? What would you do differently next time?"</em>
        Over time, children internalise the prompts, and they start asking the
        questions of themselves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 04 · How it all fits together ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <SectionEyebrow number="04" label="How it all fits together" />
      <h2 class="display">
        Three layers, <em>one rhythm</em>.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="fits-together">
      <p>
        The <strong>five domains</strong> tell us <em>what</em> we're developing
        in every child. The <strong>nine thinking processes</strong> tell us
        <em>how children think</em> when they're learning well. Our
        <strong>EXPLORE methodology</strong> is <em>how we deliver the day</em>
        so all of this happens in the rhythm of normal, joyful, child-led play.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="curriculum-ctas">
      <a href="/book-a-tour" class="btn-primary">
        Book a tour
        <ArrowIcon />
      </a>
      <a href="/philosophy" class="btn-secondary">Read our philosophy</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── References ──────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="references">
    <div class="references-inner">
      <h4>References</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Australian Early Development Census (AEDC). <em>aedc.gov.au</em></li>
        <li>
          Chatzipantelia, A., Grammatikopoulos, V., &amp; Gregoriadis, A.
          (2014). <em>Development and evaluation of metacognition in early
          childhood education.</em>
        </li>
        <li>
          Early Years Learning Framework (EYLF). (2009). <em>Australian
          Government Department of Education.</em>
        </li>
        <li>Mayesky, M. (2006). <em>Creative Activities for Young Children.</em></li>
        <li>
          NSW Government. (2020). <em>Cognitive load theory and metacognition
          resources.</em>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
